@@@ note
`[!NOTE]`-shaped text outside a blockquote entirely is just prose that happens to contain
brackets. It gets ordinary bracket escaping, same as it did before this marker handling
existed.

@@@ input
Regular prose that happens to mention [!NOTE] mid-sentence is not a blockquote at all,
so the brackets are escaped as ordinary text like any other literal bracket pair.

@@@ wrap 200
Regular prose that happens to mention \[!NOTE\] mid-sentence is not a blockquote at all, so the brackets are escaped as ordinary text like any other literal bracket pair.

@@@ unwrap
Regular prose that happens to mention \[!NOTE\] mid-sentence is not a blockquote at all, so the brackets are escaped as ordinary text like any other literal bracket pair.
